1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,002
Welcome to lesson fourteen: RBAC and ServiceAccounts.

2
00:00:04,102 --> 00:00:14,512
You'll understand who can do what in a cluster: how people and programs are identified, how permissions are granted, how apps get cloud access without keys, and which permissions are dangerous.

3
00:00:15,862 --> 00:00:19,216
Every request to the API server passes three gates.

4
00:00:19,316 --> 00:00:26,398
Authentication asks who you are. Authorization asks whether you're allowed. Admission checks whether the object is acceptable.

5
00:00:26,498 --> 00:00:32,940
A four oh one means Kubernetes doesn't know who you are. A four oh three means it does, but RBAC said no.

6
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:44,250
Kubernetes has no user objects. People come from certificates, single sign-on, or cloud identity. ServiceAccounts are the exception: real objects, used by programs in Pods.

7
00:00:45,600 --> 00:00:49,610
An RBAC rule names an API group, resources, and verbs.

8
00:00:49,710 --> 00:00:58,856
The core group, written as an empty string, holds Pods, Services, ConfigMaps and Secrets. The apps group holds Deployments and StatefulSets.

9
00:00:58,956 --> 00:01:03,910
A wrong API group is a common mistake. Deployments under the core group grant nothing at all.

10
00:01:05,260 --> 00:01:11,334
Verbs are actions: get, list, watch, create, update, patch and delete.

11
00:01:11,434 --> 00:01:19,396
Some actions are separate subresources that you must grant explicitly. Reading logs is pods slash log. Exec is pods slash exec.

12
00:01:19,496 --> 00:01:23,922
So permission to get Pods does not allow reading their logs or running commands in them.

13
00:01:25,272 --> 00:01:28,338
Roles and bindings combine in a few ways.

14
00:01:28,438 --> 00:01:40,000
A Role with a RoleBinding works in one namespace. A ClusterRole with a RoleBinding reuses one definition, but only in that namespace. A ClusterRole with a ClusterRoleBinding applies everywhere.

15
00:01:40,100 --> 00:01:50,798
Kubernetes has built-in roles for people. view reads most things, but not Secrets. edit can change most things, and can read Secrets. admin also manages permissions in the namespace.

16
00:01:52,148 --> 00:01:54,222
A few rules about permissions.

17
00:01:54,322 --> 00:02:00,412
They only add up. There are no deny rules, so to take a permission away, you change the binding that grants it.

18
00:02:00,512 --> 00:02:09,674
A binding's role reference can't be edited. ServiceAccount subjects need a namespace. And prefer groups from your identity provider over individual users.

19
00:02:09,774 --> 00:02:14,568
Never put people in the system masters group, which bypasses RBAC completely.

20
00:02:15,918 --> 00:02:24,744
Every namespace has a default ServiceAccount, and Pods use it unless told otherwise. Don't grant it permissions, or every Pod in the namespace gets them.

21
00:02:24,844 --> 00:02:31,398
Create one ServiceAccount per app, and turn off token mounting unless the app actually calls the Kubernetes API.

22
00:02:32,748 --> 00:02:38,422
Modern ServiceAccount tokens are short-lived, refreshed automatically, and bound to a specific Pod.

23
00:02:38,522 --> 00:02:48,900
Old tokens stored in Secrets never expire, and since version one twenty-four they aren't created automatically. Advice to get the token from the ServiceAccount's Secret is outdated.

24
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:57,890
For cloud access, link a ServiceAccount to a cloud identity, such as EKS Pod Identity. Then no static access keys are stored anywhere.

25
00:02:59,240 --> 00:03:01,810
Some permissions are much more powerful than they look.

26
00:03:01,910 --> 00:03:09,168
Listing Secrets returns every password. Creating Pods lets someone run as any ServiceAccount in the namespace and mount any Secret.

27
00:03:09,268 --> 00:03:15,646
And bind, escalate and impersonate bypass RBAC's built-in protection against granting yourself more power.

28
00:03:16,996 --> 00:03:24,846
Test permissions with kubectl auth can-i. With dash dash as, you can check what someone else can do, including a ServiceAccount.

29
00:03:24,946 --> 00:03:29,132
Dash dash list shows everything a subject can do in a namespace.

30
00:03:30,482 --> 00:03:34,908
In the lab, you'll create a read-only role and test exactly what it allows.

31
00:03:35,008 --> 00:03:40,250
You'll call the API from inside a Pod using its token, and see Secrets forbidden.

32
00:03:40,350 --> 00:03:46,328
And you'll watch RBAC block an escalation attempt, then succeed once the dangerous bind verb is granted.

33
00:03:47,678 --> 00:03:48,936
Let's recap.

34
00:03:49,036 --> 00:03:56,694
Requests are authenticated, authorized and admitted. Use the right API groups, and remember subresources for logs and exec.

35
00:03:56,794 --> 00:04:02,612
Give each app its own ServiceAccount with least privilege, and use workload identity for cloud access.

36
00:04:02,712 --> 00:04:08,674
And guard the dangerous permissions closely. Next, you'll lock down the network and Pod security.
